# What `docker build` may see. `COPY . .` copies everything that is not listed here into the
# image, and an image is easy to push, share and pull apart: a secret that reaches a layer has
# left the machine. So the rule is the reverse of .gitignore's — list what the running service
# does not need, starting with anything that could hold a secret or someone's data.

# Secrets. They reach the container as environment variables at run time, never through a layer.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/*.crt
**/*.p12
**/*.pfx
**/id_rsa*
**/id_ed25519*

# Runtime data: the database and its archive of every log line, exports, dead letters, spool,
# file-tail offsets, and the third-party sample corpora. The container gets its own data volume.
data/*
!data/samples/
.data/
suricata-logs/

# Version control, CI and editor state
.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
**/.DS_Store

# Local Python environments, caches and build output
venv/
.venv/
env/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
build/
dist/

# Not part of the running service: tests, documentation, notes, recordings, the leftovers of the
# archived legacy app, and the files that describe the image itself
tests/
docs/
Claude outputs/
*.md
!README.md
*.mp4
*.mov
core/
api/
ingest/
rca/
storage/
ui/
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
